  "literature": {
   "diosi_2007": "arXiv:quant-ph/0607110 (J. Phys. A 40, 2989), Eqs. 8-9, 12-13: \"omega_G^2 = (4 pi/3M) G int f^2(r) dr ... At constant mass density omega_G^2 = (4pi/3) G fbar = G M/R^3\"; decoherence time \"2 hbar R^3/(G M^2) 1/|x-y|^2\". => Gamma = hbar omega_G^2/M = (4pi/3) G hbar/V (the int f^2 form IS the V_eff generalisation). Original: Diosi, PRA 40, 1165 (1989).",
   "diosi_2013": "arXiv:1302.5365, Eq. 9-10: omega_G = sqrt(4 pi G rho/3) \"does not depend on the size but on the density\"; and proves the c.o.m. rate is insensitive to the spread of nuclear positions (our lattice check).",
   "nhh_2014": "arXiv:1405.2868 (Nimmrichter, Hornberger, Hammerer PRL 113, 020405), Eq. 11: \"D_DP = (G hbar/2pi^2) int d^3k (k_x^2/k^2)|rho~(k)|^2\" - the general shape-dependent form (our demagnetization-tensor reading); the G=0 term is the volume law.",
   "oppenheim_2023": "arXiv:2203.01982 (Nat Commun 14, 7910), Sec. V discussion: \"the variation in acceleration depends on 1/sqrt(N) the number of nucleons in the test mass, so that masses with smaller volume should yield larger uncertainty and this would be the effect to look for\" - volume scaling already proposed as the CQ signature.",
   "tilloy_diosi_2017": "arXiv:1706.01856 Eq. 11 D = gamma/4 + V o gamma^-1 o V^T; Eq. 13-14 PLD gamma = -2V -> D = G/|x-y| (Diosi kappa=1). Confirms the Stage 26 (kappa + 1/4kappa) structure and min at kappa=1/2."
